{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AC4 pushed for review: https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/c/rebar/+/2608 (commit f50dc1007bd0, Change-Id I55bc93a0a81b4dcd011c733b02e6d9dac3a9cf34).\n\nSCOPE OF THIS CHANGE: AC4 ONLY. rebar doctor gains a build-freshness section reading two independent local signals -- reject-streak (the updater's own counter vs its threshold of 3) and build-stale (the build behind the current pointer more than 25 commits behind origin/main). No network, no credential, no CI provider. Advisory: printed in text and JSON, outside doctor's exit code and outside --repair, on the same grounds doctor already applies to HOME-sourced MCP-client findings. Absent updater state emits one 'unavailable' finding, never a failure.\n\nFiles: src/rebar/_commands/doctor_build_freshness.py (new), src/rebar/_commands/doctor.py (wiring), src/rebar/llm/build_drift.py (optional build_sha so the EXISTING ancestry walk can measure a build this process is not executing -- reuse, not a second walk), tests/unit/test_doctor_build_freshness.py (new, RED-first), docs/user-guide.md.\n\nNO NEW MECHANISM. A scripts/check_*.py would have registered a ci_gate named by its path (scripts/_mechanism_delta/detect_ci.py::_detect_gate_scripts), which --check admits via a marker but tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py rejects regardless (the contradiction filed as a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a). Delivering this inside the existing rebar doctor command avoids the kind entirely: check_mechanism_delta.py --check reports new=0 increased=0 stale=0 and test_mechanism_delta.py passes 31/31. No marker added, no baseline edited.\n\nAn XDG_STATE_HOME read was REMOVED rather than marked: check_config_ownership.py flagged it as an unresolved-env-read below an approved seam, and the updater does not consult that variable anyway, so the code was accommodating a layout that is never written. The module now reads no environment at all.\n\nGATES: review-plan PASS and SIGNED at head_sha f50dc1007bd0 (verified_at_sha 6125c5345f01). Three runs: run 1 blocked deterministically in 6s with no LLM spend (P1 readiness-shape, P10 verification-presence); run 2 blocked on one LLM finding (AC4 named no staleness threshold); run 3 passed. The description was rewritten to the standard checklist shape and the incident forensics moved to the preceding comment -- it was at 7,556 of the 8,000-char admission cap with 444 chars of headroom, so the required structure could not be added without moving narrative out. file_impact recorded across the 5 files.\n\nVerification: make lint exit 0 (ruff, complexity new=0 increased=0, mechanism new=0, config-ownership, uv-pin, zizmor, actionlint); make typecheck clean over 755 files; 110 targeted tests + 102 regression tests pass. On this host the detector reports the live bug: reject-streak 122, build-stale 201 commits, both climbing hourly.\n\nSTILL OPEN: AC1 and AC2 (with the operator as an unapplied diff to the machine-local, git-ignored rebar-dev-update.sh -- outside any repository test's reach) and AC5 (pruner Directory-not-empty + the 5fb69334...1559 stub). AC3 withdrawn by operator decision. NOT closing this ticket.\n\nCarried advisory from run 3, recorded rather than actioned: build_drift.detect_drift performs no fetch, so origin/main is whatever the local checkout last fetched -- a checkout that has not fetched recently UNDERSTATES drift. That is the conservative direction (it never invents drift) and is documented at the TRACKED_REF constant, but a future change could resolve the ref via a bounded best-effort git ls-remote degrading to unavailable."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788495972186861001,"uuid":"18dee2cb-503f-4a5d-9747-f10bc7317b35"}